pages["cash-vs-inflation.html"] = page("cash-vs-inflation.html", 'Cash vs Inflation: Why Idle Savings Lose Value (UK)',
  "How inflation eats the buying power of cash, a 100-year UK picture, a worked example and a sensible order for growing money long term.",
  '<div class="page-head"><div class="wrap"><div class="crumb"><a href="index.html">Home</a> › <a href="how-money-works.html">Learn</a> › Cash vs inflation</div><h1>Why cash alone keeps you poorer: how inflation eats your savings</h1><p>Why money left in cash loses buying power, and the sensible order to grow it over the long term. Information, not financial advice.</p></div></div><div class="wrap"><style>figure{margin:1rem 0}figure img{max-width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid #d7e3df}figcaption{font-size:.85rem;color:#555;margin-top:.4rem}#sources+ul a{overflow-wrap:anywhere}</style>\n<div class="cols"><div>\n<p>I want to start with something that surprised me when I first worked it out. Money sitting still isn\'t really sitting still. If it\'s in a drawer, a current account, or a savings account paying less than inflation, it buys a bit less every year. Nobody takes it from you. Prices just creep up, and your pounds stretch less far.</p>\n<p>That\'s why I think understanding money matters so much. Saving is a great habit. But if your long-term money only ever sits in cash, it can quietly lose ground. Over the long run, investing sensibly is how most people grow their wealth.</p>\n<h2 id="what">What inflation actually is</h2>\n<p><strong>Inflation</strong> is the rate at which prices rise over time. If inflation is 3%, something that cost £100 a year ago costs about £103 today. Your £100 note hasn\'t changed, but it now buys a little less. Economists call this losing <strong>purchasing power</strong> (or buying power).</p>\n<p>The Bank of England tries to keep inflation at 2% a year. Right now it\'s above that. The Office for National Statistics\' latest figure shows Consumer Prices Index (CPI) inflation at <strong>3.5% in the 12 months to September 2026</strong>, and the Bank of England\'s interest rate (<strong>Bank Rate</strong>) is <strong>3.75%</strong>. Bank Rate affects the interest banks pay on savings, so it\'s worth checking whether your own savings rate is actually beating inflation. Many easy-access accounts and most current accounts don\'t.</p>\n<h2 id="century">The 100-year picture</h2>\n<p>Here\'s what a century of rising prices looks like.</p>\n<figure><img alt="Chart: the pound has lost 99% of its value in 100 years. £1 from 1926 had about 1.2p of buying power by 2025, because UK prices (ONS Retail Prices Index) rose about 86 times. Milestones: 1946 69p (£1.45 to buy what £1 bought in 1926), 1966 30p (£3.30), 1986 4.7p (£21), 2006 2.4p (£43), 2025 1.2p (£86); buying power peaked at 117p in 1934 when prices were falling." src="assets/pound-99pct-100y.png" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy"><figcaption>What £1 from 1926 buys now, and what 1926’s £1 of shopping costs today. Sources: ONS Retail Prices Index long-run series (CDKO); Bank of England millennium dataset. Chart: @moneybysharma.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Using the ONS\'s long-run <strong>Retail Prices Index</strong> (an older measure of how prices change), the index stood at 18.5 in 1926 and 1,588.6 in 2025. That means prices rose roughly <strong>86 times</strong> over the century. Put another way, <strong>£1 from 1926 buys only about 1.2p worth of what it did back then.</strong></p>\n<p>The pound has weakened against the US dollar too: about <strong>$4.86 in 1926</strong> compared with roughly <strong>$1.33 on 28 October 2026</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nobody keeps cash under the mattress for 100 years, of course. But the same thing happens on a smaller scale over any decade.</p>\n<h2 id="example">A simple worked example (illustrative)</h2>\n<p>Let\'s keep the maths simple. These numbers are <strong>illustrative only</strong>, not a forecast:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You hold <strong>£10,000 in cash earning 0% interest</strong>.</li>\n<li>Inflation averages <strong>3% a year</strong> for <strong>10 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After 10 years you\'d still have £10,000. But it would buy only about <strong>£7,440</strong> worth of what it buys today (£10,000 ÷ 1.03¹⁰). That\'s roughly a quarter of its buying power gone, without you spending a penny.</p>\n<p>Interest helps. If your savings rate matches or beats inflation, you keep up. The problem is cash that earns little or nothing for years at a time.</p>\n<h2 id="steps">So what can you do? A sensible order</h2>\n<p>I don\'t think the answer is "put everything into the stock market." Investing has real risks, and the order you do things in matters. Here\'s the order most guidance suggests.</p>\n<h3>1. Build an emergency fund in easy-access cash first</h3>\n<p>Cash is the right home for money you might need soon. Many guides suggest <strong>3–6 months of essential spending</strong> (rent or mortgage, bills, food, travel) in an easy-access savings account (see <a href="https://www.moneyhelper.org.uk/en/savings/types-of-savings/emergency-savings-how-much-is-enough" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MoneyHelper on emergency savings</a>). This is your safety net, so you\'re never forced to sell investments at a bad time or reach for a credit card. Shop around for a decent rate. See my guide to <a href="budgeting-emergency-fund.html#emergency">budgeting and emergency funds</a>.</p>\n<h3>2. Pay off expensive debt</h3>\n<p>If you\'re paying 20% or more on a credit card, clearing it is a guaranteed "return" that\'s very hard to beat by investing. Deal with high-interest debt before you invest. See <a href="credit-scores-debt-help.html">credit scores and debt help</a>.</p>\n<h3>3. Invest money you won\'t need for at least 5 years</h3>\n<p><strong>Investing</strong> means buying a share in things like companies (<strong>shares</strong>, also called equities) or lending to governments and firms (<strong>bonds</strong>). Over long periods, shares have historically tended to grow faster than inflation, but there\'s no guarantee. Prices can fall sharply, sometimes for years.</p>\n<p><strong>The value of investments can go down as well as up, and you may get back less than you put in.</strong></p>\n<p>That\'s why time matters. Money you\'ll need within five years usually belongs in cash. Money for 10, 20 or 30 years from now has time to ride out the bad patches.</p>\n<h3>4. Use tax wrappers</h3>\n<p>A <strong>tax wrapper</strong> is like a protective box around your money that shields it from tax.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stocks &amp; Shares ISA:</strong> you can put in up to £20,000 a year (across all your ISAs) and pay no UK tax on the growth or income, and none when you take money out.</li>\n<li><strong>Pensions:</strong> you get tax relief on what you pay in, and workplace pensions often come with your employer\'s contributions on top. That\'s extra money. The trade-off is that you can normally only access it <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/increasing-normal-minimum-pension-age" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">from age 55 (rising to 57 from 6 April 2028)</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More detail in my guides to <a href="investments.html#isa">investments and ISAs</a> and <a href="pensions.html">pensions</a>.</p>\n<h3>5. Keep it simple, low-cost and spread out</h3>\n<p>You don\'t need to pick individual shares. Many people use <strong>low-cost, diversified funds</strong>. A <strong>fund</strong> pools money from lots of investors to buy many different investments at once. <strong>Diversified</strong> means spread out, so one company or country having a bad year doesn\'t sink you. <strong>Index funds</strong> (also called tracker funds) simply follow a whole market, and they usually have low fees.</p>\n<p>Fees matter more than they look. A 1% difference each year compounds over decades, just like returns do. See <a href="power-of-compounding.html">the power of compounding</a>.</p>\n<h2 id="bottom">The honest bottom line</h2>\n<p>Cash is essential for your emergency fund and short-term plans. But cash you won\'t touch for many years, earning less than inflation, is quietly getting poorer. Understanding how money works, and putting long-term money to work sensibly, is how most people build wealth over time.</p>\n<p>Investing isn\'t the only option and it\'s never guaranteed. If you\'re unsure, the government-backed <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.moneyhelper.org.uk/">MoneyHelper</a> service offers free guidance, or you can speak to an FCA-authorised financial adviser.</p>\n<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>ONS – CPI annual rate (D7G7), September 2026: 3.5% (released 21 October 2026): <a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/timeseries/d7g7/mm23" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/timeseries/d7g7/mm23</a></li>\n<li>Bank of England – Bank Rate 3.75%, inflation target 2%: <a href="https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy/the-interest-rate-bank-rate" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy/the-interest-rate-bank-rate</a></li>\n<li>ONS – Retail Prices Index long-run series (CDKO): <a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/timeseries/cdko/mm23" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/timeseries/cdko/mm23</a></li>\n<li>Bank of England – A millennium of macroeconomic data (GBP/USD 1926): <a href="https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/statistics/research-datasets" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/statistics/research-datasets</a></li>\n<li>GOV.UK – Individual Savings Accounts (ISA allowance £20,000, 2026 to 2027 tax year): <a href="https://www.gov.uk/individual-savings-accounts" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.gov.uk/individual-savings-accounts</a></li>\n<li>GOV.UK – Increasing normal minimum pension age (55 to 57 from 6 April 2028): <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/increasing-normal-minimum-pension-age" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/increasing-normal-minimum-pension-age</a></li>\n<li>MoneyHelper – How much to save for an emergency (3–6 months of essential outgoings): <a href="https://www.moneyhelper.org.uk/en/savings/types-of-savings/emergency-savings-how-much-is-enough" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.moneyhelper.org.uk/en/savings/types-of-savings/emergency-savings-how-much-is-enough</a></li>\n<li>ECB reference rates via Frankfurter (GBP/USD, 28 October 2026): <a href="https://www.frankfurter.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.frankfurter.app/</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Information, not financial advice.</em></p>\n</div><aside><div class="card toc"><h3>On this page</h3><a href="#what">What inflation actually is</a><a href="#century">The 100-year picture</a><a href="#example">A simple worked example</a><a href="#steps">So what can you do? A sensible order</a><a href="#bottom">The honest bottom line</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></div></aside></div></div>')
